An **open end** removes one $\pi r^{2}$, so an open-top cylinder is $\pi r^{2} + 2\pi rh$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw (-3,0)--(-3,8);
\draw (3,0)--(3,8) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\draw (0,8) ellipse (3 and 0.9);
\draw (0,8) ellipse (2.5 and 0.75);
\draw (-3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=3,y radius=0.9];
\draw[dashed] (-3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,x radius=3,y radius=0.9];
\fill (0,8) circle (2pt);
\draw (0,8)--(3,8) node[midway,above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **pipe** open at both ends keeps no circular ends, leaving just the curved surface $2\pi rh$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.35]
\draw (0,-4)--(20,-4);
\draw (0,4)--(20,4) node[midway,above] {$h$};
\draw (20,0) ellipse (1.4 and 4);
\draw (20,0) ellipse (1.05 and 3.2);
\draw (0,4) arc[start angle=90,end angle=270,x radius=1.4,y radius=4];
\draw[dashed] (0,4) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-90,x radius=1.4,y radius=4];
\fill (20,0) circle (2pt);
\draw (20,0)--(20,4) node[midway,right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **half-cylinder** keeps two semicircular ends ($2 \times \tfrac{1}{2}\pi r^{2} = \pi r^{2}$) and half the curved surface ($\pi rh$), and the flat cut exposes a rectangle of size $2r \times h$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.4]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\coordinate (A) at (-6,0);
\coordinate (B) at (6,0);
\coordinate (S) at (3,-5.1962);
\coordinate (A2) at (-1.2369,2.75);
\coordinate (B2) at (10.7631,2.75);
\coordinate (S2) at (7.7631,-2.4462);
\draw (A) arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,radius=6];
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(0,-6) node[midway,right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$r$};
\draw (A2)--(B2);
\draw[dashed] (A2) arc[start angle=180,end angle=300,radius=6];
\draw[dashed] (B2) arc[start angle=360,end angle=300,radius=6];
\draw (A)--(A2) node[midway,above left] {$h$};
\draw (B)--(B2);
\draw (S)--(S2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **quarter-cylinder** keeps two quarter ends ($\tfrac{1}{2}\pi r^{2}$) and a quarter of the curved surface ($\tfrac{1}{2}\pi rh$), and the cut exposes **two** flat rectangles ($2rh$).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.5]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (6,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (0,6);
\coordinate (O2) at (4.7631,2.75);
\coordinate (P2) at (10.7631,2.75);
\coordinate (Q2) at (4.7631,8.75);
\draw (O)--(P) node[midway,below] {$r$};
\draw (O)--(Q);
\draw (P) arc[start angle=0,end angle=90,radius=6];
\draw (P)--(P2) node[midway,below right] {$h$};
\draw (Q)--(Q2);
\draw (P2) arc[start angle=0,end angle=90,radius=6];
\draw[dashed] (O)--(O2);
\draw[dashed] (O2)--(P2);
\draw[dashed] (O2)--(Q2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

